<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page2-inspection-examples" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Two More Examples: Water Pollution and Arguing over Marbles</title>

  <p>
    On this page we explore two more games and their equilibria. We start with
    the Water Pollution game, which has three players. This makes the analysis
    a little different because we can't just look at one table.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-3">
    <title>Section 3.3, Water Pollution 1</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Read this one and try to figure out whether each of the four
      possibilities discussed is a Nash equilibrium before the book tells you
      the answer! After this, the book gives further context on these
      so-called <q>free-rider</q> problems.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="check-water-pollution">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The profiles where two firms purify and one pollutes are Nash
        equilibria. In those profiles the polluter gets <m>0</m> while each
        purifier gets <m>-1</m>. Why can't a purifier improve its own payoff
        by switching to polluting?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because two firms would then be polluting, which triggers the
            treatment cost for everybody, and the switcher's payoff would drop
            from <m>-1</m> to <m>-3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the free-rider problem in miniature. The
            purifiers can see that the polluter is doing better than they are,
            and neither of them can do anything about it alone. Notice also
            that the profile where all three firms pollute is a Nash
            equilibrium, so this game has four of them.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because once a profile is a Nash equilibrium, the definition does
            not allow a player to change strategy.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The definition never forbids anything. It says that if a player
            were to change strategy on their own, they would not end up better
            off. Working out what would happen after such a change is exactly
            how we test a profile.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because purifying is a dominant strategy for every firm.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            It is not. If the other two firms both purify, a firm does better
            by polluting, since it saves the cost of purifying and the lake
            stays clean. That is precisely why the profile where all three
            purify fails to be an equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    And now it's time to... argue over marbles!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-4-setup">
    <title>Section 3.4, Arguing over Marbles: the setup only</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the beginning of Section 3.4, stopping after the list of
      payoffs.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the paragraph describing the two children and the three bulleted
      rules for the payoffs. Stop when the book begins inspecting strategy
      profiles. We will do that part below, and then you will read the book's
      version afterwards to compare.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Before we begin the analysis, take a moment to make sure you understand
    the payoff rules by exploring them with the interactive below. Move either
    child's number and watch which rule applies.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-marbles-explorer">
    <caption>
      How the payoffs work. The argument ends when the first child gives up,
      so it lasts as long as the smaller of the two numbers.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-marbles-explorer"
      iframe="ch3-graph/marbles-payoff-explorer.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
      <static>
        <p>
          A number line from 0 to 1 carrying both children's numbers, with a
          slider for each. A marker shows where the argument ends, at whichever
          number is smaller, and a sentence reports who gets the marbles. Both
          payoffs are displayed with the current numbers substituted in, and a
          table of the three payoff rules highlights whichever rule is in force.
          Moving one child's number past the other switches the highlighted
          rule and reverses who wins the marbles.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Time to look for some Nash equilibria! What makes this a bit harder is
    that there are infinitely many options for <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>. We follow
    the book's analysis, which essentially breaks into five different cases.
    Reading the book's reasoning can be confusing if you don't think it through
    on your own first! So we will look at a few cases together, and you can
    think through the rest on your own.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the widget below you will explore three subcases of <m>s \lt t</m>. In
    each, decide whether either child can do better by changing her own number.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-marbles-s-lt-t">
    <caption>
      Inspecting the profiles with <m>s \lt t</m>.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-marbles-s-lt-t"
      iframe="ch3-graph/marbles-s-less-than-t.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          A three step walkthrough of the region where Player 1's number is
          smaller than Player 2's. Each step shows a number line with the two
          numbers positioned for that case, a shaded band marking which choices
          keep a profile inside the case, and the payoffs written symbolically.
          A multiple choice question asks which child, if any, can improve. The
          first two cases fail the definition, and the third, the single profile
          with <m>s = 0</m> and <m>t = 1</m>, is a Nash equilibrium. It is not a
          strict one, since Player 2 has other numbers that pay her exactly as
          well.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    So we have concluded that for <m>s \lt t</m>, the only Nash equilibrium is
    <m>(s, t) = (0, 1)</m>. See if you can work out the following before
    reading on:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          For <m>t \lt s</m>, can you use the symmetry of the payoff functions
          to quickly deduce the single equilibrium, without going through all
          three subcases again?
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          For <m>s = t</m>, do any Nash equilibria exist in the following
          cases?
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                When <m>s = t \lt 1</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                When <m>s = t = 1</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-4-rest">
    <title>The Rest of Section 3.4</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 3.4.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      You have already done this reasoning, so move through the case analysis
      quickly and use it to check your own work against the book's. After the
      case analysis, you can read the book's discussion of the War of
      Attrition.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-5">
    <title>Section 3.5, Tobacco Market (optional)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Section 3.5 is optional. Read it if you are interested, or skip
      it.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      A fun econ one, if that's your thing.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>